//! Connectors.
//!
//! Every connector provides the generic `Connector` (ADR 0068), which says it is one: the schematic
//! and the placer find connectors by it, and a project's own connector provides it too.
//!
//! Every pin header and socket states `.mating`, what a connector says about how it meets
//! another: its kind, gender, pitch, rows and positions. A `system` joins two boards by mating
//! their connectors (`.carrier.slot.mating ~ .card.j1.mating`, ADR 0040): contact N of one is
//! contact N of the other, and a header mates with a socket. Contact N of a part is its pin
//! `pN`, and pin 1 is the one the part's footprint marks; a header and its socket number so that
//! contact N meets contact N.
//!
//! `.pin_current` is the rated current of one contact. It is not always true of a part (it depends
//! on the maker), so a part states it only through a `header` catalog row, whose `source` is the
//! datasheet; a part without a row is unrated, and the check of the current through a mate
//! lists it as not evaluated. Only the single-row parts have rows (`data/jlcpcb/README.md`).
//!
//! `.seat` is how far above its board's face the partner rests (ADR 0110), also only through a
//! `header` row (an `optional` column: a project's row may leave it empty). Two mated seats add
//! up to the mate's mated height, the distance between the two boards' faces.
//!
//! There is one definition for each size of header (ADR 0023): the language has no parameter for a
//! count, and a part's pin rows are written out. The sizes are single-row 2, 4, 8 and 10, the same
//! sockets, and 2x3, 2x5 and 2x10; other sizes are added when a design needs one. Pins are `pN` on
//! pad N whatever the rows are, and the footprint decides which pin is in which row. A socket is
//! its own kind because it is a different symbol and bill-of-materials line from a header. The
//! pitch is the package (`.package &= "2.54mm"`); the names a package can take are the mapping's.
//! The USB Type-C pad numbers were first written from knowledge of the specification, and the
//! mapping (ADR 0021) checked them against KiCad's receptacle symbols and footprints, which
//! corrected two things: a power-only receptacle has six contacts (see `UsbCPower`), and the
//! sixteen-contact USB 2.0 receptacle has the sideband pads A8 and B8 (see `UsbC2`).

import std::bus::{Usb2, UsbRole}
import std::parts::passive::Resistor
import std::sources::Rail

/// What a connector says about how it meets another (ADR 0040). A system mates two connectors
/// with `~` between their `.mating`; the checker compares the two and reads each board's own
/// wiring behind the contacts.
interface Mating {
    /// What sort of connector: `pin_header` (headers and sockets). The kinds that need more than
    /// a contact-for-contact join (flat cables, wire to board) are not modelled.
    .kind: String
    /// `header` or `socket`: a header mates with a socket, never with a header.
    .gender: String
    /// The distance between contacts in a row.
    .pitch: Length
    .rows: Number
    /// How many contacts: the part's pin count.
    .positions: Number
    /// The rated current of one contact. A catalog column; unset means unrated.
    .pin_current: Current
    /// How far above the face of its board this connector's partner rests when the two are
    /// mated (ADR 0110): a header's plastic top, a socket's body top. It is a fact of the
    /// part, whatever it mates with, so a mate's mated height is the two seats added, with
    /// both tolerances. It depends on the maker's drawing, so a part states it only through a
    /// `header` catalog row, whose `source` names the drawing; unset means unknown, and a
    /// mate with an unknown seat has no mated height. Never a default and never the part's
    /// `.height` (a maximum, not a seat).
    .seat: Length
    /// `"none"`, or what keys the housing so that it goes in one way (`"shrouded"`,
    /// `"polarised"`). Unset is the same as `"none"`. A mate whose pinout would also work turned
    /// end for end warns unless a connector is keyed.
    .keyed: String
}

/// What a connector is: a part that is the way in and out of a board, so its reference
/// designator starts with `J`. It states only what is true of every connector. Every standard
/// connector below provides it, and so does a connector of a project (`part MolexPicoBlade
/// provides Connector { ... }`, ADR 0068). The schematic and the placer ask a part for this
/// generic to know that it is a connector, never its name or its designator. It has no pads and
/// no catalog, so it is no part until a provider stands for it.
generic Connector {
    .designator &= "J"

    pins {
    }
}

/// A single-row header with two pins. It is where a board's wires attach.
part Header1x2 provides Connector {
    pins {
        .p1 passive "P1" "1"
        .p2 passive "P2" "2"
    }

    .mating: Mating {
        .kind &= "pin_header"
        .gender &= "header"
        .pitch &= 2.54mm
        .rows &= 1
        .positions &= 2
    }

    .package: Package
    catalog header { .mating.gender, .mating.pitch, .mating.rows, .mating.positions, .mating.pin_current, .mating.seat optional }
}

/// A single-row header with 4 pins, `p1` to `p4`, pin N on pad N.
part Header1x4 provides Connector {
    pins {
        .p1 passive "P1" "1"
        .p2 passive "P2" "2"
        .p3 passive "P3" "3"
        .p4 passive "P4" "4"
    }

    .mating: Mating {
        .kind &= "pin_header"
        .gender &= "header"
        .pitch &= 2.54mm
        .rows &= 1
        .positions &= 4
    }

    .package: Package
    catalog header { .mating.gender, .mating.pitch, .mating.rows, .mating.positions, .mating.pin_current, .mating.seat optional }
}

